CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: it argues a position. The position is that the oracles against the nations are not one prophecy on one timeline. Each names a ground, a term and an ending, and the text sorts them into three classes. Some are closed, because the sentence is final and no clause of return is attached. Some are part-fulfilled, because the text itself reports what happened and what remains. Some are open, because they carry a restoration clause that no later passage reports fulfilled. The volume has argued against three readings that flatten the difference: that all are fulfilled, that all are spiritualized into the church, and that all are still to come."),
("p", "Nineveh is the plainest closed case. The forty days were suspended when the city turned, and the sentence in Nahum and Zephaniah that follows carries no clause of return. Egypt and Tyre are the plainest part-fulfilled cases. Ezekiel reports the forty years and the base kingdom for Egypt, and he reports in his own later word what the siege of Tyre did and did not bring. Moab, Ammon, Elam and the end of Egypt are open, because Jeremiah 48:47, 49:6 and 49:39 and Isaiah 19:23–25 state a further word and Scripture reports none of them done. The volume holds them open as nations blessed through Israel’s restored line in the Kingdom, with Jerusalem at its centre."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What is an oracle against a nation?", "A word from the LORD to a named nation with a ground, a term and an ending", "Jer 1:10; 18:7–10; 25:15–17"),
    ("What are the grounds?", "Violence, betrayal, gloating, pride and idols; the LORD’s concern is wider than Israel", "Amos 1:3–2:3; 9:7; Obad 10–14"),
    ("Which are closed?", "Nineveh; Damascus in its terms; Philistia, with an open remnant", "Jonah 3; Nah 1, 3; Zeph 2:13–15"),
    ("Which are part-fulfilled?", "Egypt and Tyre: the text reports what happened and what remains", "Ezek 29:13–20; 26; Isa 23:15–18"),
    ("Which are open?", "Moab, Ammon, Elam and the end of Egypt, by their own restoration clauses", "Jer 48:47; 49:6, 39; Isa 19:23–25"),
    ("What ties the oracles together?", "A measure cut to the ground, and a cup and a day", "Jer 25:12–33; Obad 15; Joel 3:1–8"),
    ("Who is in the open clauses?", "Nations blessed through Israel’s restored line; the Body is not in them", "Isa 2:2–4; Zech 8:20–23; 14:16–21"),
  ], [0.28, 0.44, 0.28]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. It has granted that Ferguson and Hanegraaff are right in the main for Tyre, that the charge of failure rests on a reading of Ezekiel 29:17–20 that the verses do not require, and that the miracle of Isaiah 19 is rightly marvelled at by those who read it of the church. It has said where it knows a writer’s position only through a report. Peels and Criswell support parts of the argument, and the volume has said where it differs from them. It has left Babylon, Edom, the court of Matthew 25 and the war of Ezekiel 38 and 39 to the places that read them. What it hopes the reader takes away is a habit: before saying that an oracle against a nation is fulfilled, or future, or about the church, to ask what the oracle names as its ground, what it names as its term, and what it says comes after."),
]

